#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:0
msgid ""
"Run snap applications, inspect available commands, manage default and manual "
"aliases, and choose preferred aliases when commands conflict."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:8
msgid "Apps and aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:10
msgid ""
"When installing a snap, you're often installing an application. If an "
"application uses a GUI, it will be integrated into your desktop environment "
"and it can be launched just like any other GUI application."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:12
msgid ""
"But applications installed from a snap can also be run from the command "
"line, regardless of whether they're GUI-based or terminal-based."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:14
msgid ""
"The following sections describe how to work with such snap applications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:16
msgid "Running applications"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:18
msgid ""
"As covered in {ref}`Get started <tutorials-get-started>`, a snap's installed "
"application(s) can be found under `/snap/bin`, and subsequently, often added "
"to _$PATH_. This makes commands directly accessible from the command line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:20
msgid ""
"If executing a command directly doesn't work, try prefixing it with the */"
"snap/bin* path:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:22
msgid "$ /snap/bin/vlc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:25
msgid ""
"Adding `/snap/bin` to your default _$PATH_ makes running snaps that don't "
"automatically add themselves more convenient."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:29
msgid ""
"A few Linux distributions use a path of `/var/lib/snapd/snap/bin` instead of "
"the standard `/snap/bin`. You may need to experiment and update your path "
"accordingly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:32
msgid "Application commands"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:34
msgid ""
"The output of `snap info` lists the applications a snap makes available "
"beneath the *commands:* section:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:36
msgid ""
"$ snap info vlc\n"
"name:      vlc\n"
"summary:   The ultimate media player\n"
"(...)\n"
"commands:\n"
"  - vlc\n"
"(...)\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:46
msgid ""
"To avoid the potential conflict of snaps exposing applications with the same "
"name, every snap has a namespace reserved for itself.  The namespace uses "
"the following syntax:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:48
msgid "`<snap name>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:49
msgid "`<snap name>.<application name>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:51
msgid ""
"The first option is used automatically when the exposed application name "
"matches the name of the  snap, such as with *vlc*. The second option is used "
"for every other case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:53
msgid "<h2 id='heading--aliases'>Application aliases</h2>\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:55
msgid ""
"The namespace syntax (see above) is effective at preventing conflicts, but "
"it's not ideal when applications have well known names that third-party "
"scripts depend upon. This is where *aliases* are useful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:57
msgid ""
"For example, [LXD](https://canonical.com/lxd) snap exposes the application "
"as *lxd*. However, many third-party scripts depend on this being  *lxc*. To "
"solve this issue, the LXD snap creates an alias for *lxc*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:59
msgid "Aliases can be viewed with the `snap aliases` command:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:61
msgid ""
"$ snap aliases lxd\n"
"Command  Alias  Notes\n"
"lxd.lxc  lxc    -\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:66
msgid ""
"Unlike applications that use a snap's namespace, there is no guarantee that "
"an alias is going to be unique. For this reason, aliases can be easily "
"disabled and re-enabled with the `unalias` and `prefer` commands:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:68
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap unalias lxd\n"
"Removed:\n"
"  - lxd.lxc as lxc\n"
"\n"
"$ sudo snap prefer lxd \n"
"Added:\n"
"  - lxd.lxc as lxc\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:78
msgid ""
"The *prefer* command enables all aliases for a given snap in preference to "
"conflicting aliases from other snaps. This allows you to switch application "
"preference between alternative options provided by other snaps."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:80
msgid ""
"A snap can also be installed with an additional `--unaliased` argument to "
"prevent aliases being created automatically:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:82
msgid ""
"$ snap install lxd --unaliased\n"
"lxd 4.22 from canonical✓ installed\n"
"$ snap aliases lxd\n"
"Command  Alias  Notes\n"
"lxd.lxc  lxc    disabled\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:90
msgid "Requesting aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:92
msgid ""
"As there is no guarantee that an alias is going to be unique, and potential "
"conflicts create a minor inconvenience, a snap's *default aliases* are only "
"enabled after a simple public review process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:94
msgid ""
"The same review process also ensures that any default commands exposed by a "
"snap are either named after the snap or, when different, reviewed to ensure "
"the alias is relevant within the context of the application. This helps "
"maintain a sane shared namespace for everyone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:96
msgid ""
"If you want an alias enabled for your snap, please open a forum topic [in "
"the store requests forum category](https://forum.snapcraft.io/c/store-"
"requests) with the details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:98
msgid "Manual aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:100
msgid ""
"Snap's aliasing system supports the creation of arbitrary custom aliases "
"defined for the local system. This allows applications to be exposed under "
"any name that is convenient locally."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:102
msgid ""
"For example, the following exposes a new command under `/snap/bin` to "
"support calling the `vlc` application as `my-vlc`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:104
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap alias vlc my-vlc\n"
"Added:\n"
"  - vlc as my-vlc\n"
"\n"
"$ snap aliases vlc\n"
"Command  Alias   Notes\n"
"vlc      my-vlc  manual\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:114
msgid ""
"When a manual alias is set, the original application name will continue to "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:116
msgid "Removing a manually created alias is also straightforward:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../how-to-guides/manage-snaps/apps-and-aliases.md:118
msgid ""
"$ sudo snap unalias my-vlc    \n"
"Removed:\n"
"  - vlc as my-vlc\n"
msgstr ""
